{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And also the hornet.</b> Know that miracles are of two kinds: hidden miracles and publicized miracles. He mentioned the publicized ones in the verse above, namely where it says, ״המסות הגדולות אשר ראו עיניך והאותות והמופתים״ — 'the great trials which your eyes saw, and the signs and wonders' — and now He will mention the plague of the hornet, which is among the hidden miracles, which appears to those who see it as coming by way of nature, like the matter of rains, for in neither of them is there a change in the nature of the world, but they are a natural phenomenon. And this is the sense of וגם — 'and also' — as if to say: and also by way of nature HaShem, may He be exalted, will save you against your enemies. Therefore you shall not be dismayed because of them — meaning that one should not fear the nations, but only HaShem who dwells in your midst." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to ask about the expression לה' אלהיך — 'to HaShem your Elohim' — and about the expression בקרבך — 'in your midst.' And the Holy One, blessed be He, mentioned two praises, and they are גדול ונורא — 'great' and 'awesome' — corresponding to the two kinds of miracles: He says that He, may He be exalted, is the possessor of power and ability, great in the hidden miracles and awesome in the publicized miracles, in the manner of what He said concerning the miracle at the sea (Exodus 15:11): ״נורא תהלות עושה פלא״. Therefore He mentioned immediately after: ״את הגדולות ואת הנוראות האלה״ — 'these great and awesome things' — הגדולות for the hidden miracles, הנוראות for the publicized miracles. And the צרעה is a species of fly that injects venom. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: there were two hornets, one in the days of Moses and one in the days of Joshua, and it would strike them in their faces and blind their eyes, and they could not fight, and Israel would come and kill them. And in another place they interpreted: the hornet would blind their eyes from above and castrate them from below, as the prophet said (Amos 2:9): ״ואנכי השמדתי את האמרי מפניהם ואשמיד פריו ממעל ושרשיו מתחת״. And so Onkelos translated צרעה as ערעיתא, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: why is it called ערעיתא? Because it stands to meet a person. The Targum renders (Deuteronomy 25) אשר קרך as דערעך."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {1 "Citation reads (דברים כה), apparently Deuteronomy 25; the phrase אשר קרך appears in Deuteronomy 25:18, rendered by Onkelos as דערעך — the verse number (18) is omitted in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:44.455250815Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}